WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.800
If you're impressed by the&nbsp;
dragons on the new "Game of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:07.440
Thrones" prequel "House of the Dragon,"&nbsp;
get ready: a bigger beast is on its way.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:12.400
Showrunners have promised that we'll&nbsp;
see at least 17 dragons in total,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:17.520
including at least 9 in the first season. Two&nbsp;
episodes into the buzzy new show's first season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.640
audiences have already met a few massive&nbsp;
dragons, including Syrax and Caraxes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.440
who belong to Princess Rhaenyra Targaryen&nbsp;
and Prince Daemon Targaryen, respectively.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.400
We've also heard about Balerion, the Black Dread,&nbsp;
whose skull now rests in the cellars of the Red&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:38.160
Keep, and Dreamfyre, whose egg was chosen for&nbsp;
Rhaenyra's late brother, then stolen by Daemon.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:43.360
However, according to a fascinating tidbit&nbsp;
mentioned by the young Lady Laena Velaryon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:47.200
there's another, way bigger dragon&nbsp;
lurking somewhere out in the world.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:49.440
"Do you know where Vhagar is now?"

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:54.560
"The dragon keepers believe she made home&nbsp;
somewhere on the coast of the Narrow Sea."

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:00.240
As audiences prepare to see a legendarily&nbsp;
large Chekhov's dragon show up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:06.160
some people might be asking: what's the deal with&nbsp;
this dragon, a massive female steed named Vhagar?

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:10.720
In the original book series by George R. R.&nbsp;
Martin, "A Song of Ice and Fire," Vhagar is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:15.360
mentioned briefly by Tyrion Lannister,&nbsp;
and her basic stats are pretty intense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.920
For starters, Tyrion notes that Vhagar is so&nbsp;
massive that a soldier could easily ride a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.240
horse down her throat. Martin writes in "A&nbsp;
Game of Thrones" that Tyrion knew Vhagar's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.440
size because he saw her skull up close,&nbsp;
as Cersei saw Balerion's in Season 7:

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:34.400
"Tyrion had stood between their gaping jaws,&nbsp;
wordless and awed. You could have ridden a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.800
horse down Vhaghar's gullet, although&nbsp;
you would not have ridden it out again."

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.040
In "A Clash of Kings," Martin writes that Vhagar's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.760
breath was "so hot that it could melt a&nbsp;
knight's armor and cook the man inside."

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.760
So what is Vhagar's importance to the&nbsp;
"Game of Thrones" prequel? In Episode 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.560
King Viserys tells Lady Laena that he's not&nbsp;
100% sure where Vhagar is at the moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:59.200
but confirms that she's far too large to reside&nbsp;
in the dragon pits of King's Landing anyway.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:04.800
"But Vhagar still lives, somewhere.&nbsp;
But too large for the Dragon Pit…"

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:07.920
"Some would say too large for our world."

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.280
While it's unsettling that Viserys&nbsp;
and his advisors seem to have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:14.720
you know, lost track of an enormous&nbsp;
fire-breathing murder machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:19.200
there's little doubt that Vhagar will appear&nbsp;
in the series eventually. Some fans think this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.920
dragon, from the "Fire Will Reign" teaser, is&nbsp;
Vhagar, but that hasn't been confirmed yet.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:28.320
Martin's books also tell us that Vhagar was&nbsp;
almost as large as Balerion, and was ridden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:34.240
by one of King Aegon's sister-wives, Visenya,&nbsp;
during Aegon's Conquest — the continent-conquering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.320
campaign that made House Targaryen what it is&nbsp;
during the events of "House of the Dragon."

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:44.000
"I'm worried for all of us. They&nbsp;
say every time a Targaryen is born,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.440
the gods toss a coin and the&nbsp;
world holds its breath."﻿

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:52.000
To say much more about Vhagar's role in "Fire &amp;&nbsp;
Blood" — the source material for "House of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.960
Dragon" — would probably spoil upcoming episodes.&nbsp;
But it's safe to say that Vhagar — the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:03.840
living dragon in the world — is out there,&nbsp;
certain to swoop in at a critical moment.

